{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P76541","secondaryAccessions":["Q2MAJ1"],"uniProtkbId":"EUTL_ECOLI","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2000-05-30","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-06-10","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"1997-02-01","entryVersion":146,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":5.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Escherichia coli (strain K12)","taxonId":83333,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Gammaproteobacteria","Enterobacterales","Enterobacteriaceae","Escherichia"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Bacterial microcompartment shell protein EutL"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"BMC-T"}},{"fullName":{"value":"Ethanolamine utilization protein EutL"}}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"value":"eutL"},"synonyms":[{"value":"yffJ"}],"orderedLocusNames":[{"value":"b2439"},{"value":"JW2432"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"Q8XLZ0"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19451619"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20044574"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20851901"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"19451619"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"20044574"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"20851901"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0007744","source":"PDB","id":"3GFH"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0007744","source":"PDB","id":"3I82"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0007744","source":"PDB","id":"3MPV"}],"value":"A component of the bacterial microcompartment (BMC) shell dedicated to ethanolamine degradation (Probable). Two crystal forms have been seen; a form with a closed central pore that has 3 very small (1.1-2.2 Angstroms) channels per trimer lined by acidic and aromatic residues (PubMed:19451619, PubMed:20044574). A form with a large central pore (8-12 Angstroms) has also been seen; this is probably a functional pore which allows molecules to enter and exit the BMC in a selective, gated manner (PubMed:20044574, PubMed:20851901). Another group only sees the central pore in the presence of Zn(2+); soaking crystals in ZnCl(2) leads to dramatic conformational changes that open a central pore of about 12 Angstroms. Whether Zn(2+) binding is physiologically relevant is unclear, however it suggests a gating mechanism exists (PubMed:20851901). Ethanolamine-binding by the small channels has been hypothesized to stabilize the EutL central pore in a closed (non-transporting) state. An open pore is thought to be large enough to transport ATP and/or cobalamin (By similarity)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"value":"Amine and polyamine degradation; ethanolamine degradation"}],"commentType":"PATHWAY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19194002"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19451619"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20044574"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20851901"}],"value":"Homotrimerizes to form a pseudohexamer (PubMed:19194002, PubMed:19451619, PubMed:20044574, PubMed:20851901). The trimers form a two-dimensional array about 37 Angstroms thick (PubMed:19451619, PubMed:20044574)"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"commentType":"INTERACTION","interactions":[{"interactantOne":{"uniProtKBAccession":"P76541","intActId":"EBI-1128826"},"interactantTwo":{"uniProtKBAccession":"P76541","geneName":"eutL","intActId":"EBI-1128826"},"numberOfExperiments":3,"organismDiffer":false}]},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"19451619"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"20044574"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"20851901"}],"value":"Bacterial microcompartment","id":"SL-0544"}}]},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"19451619"}],"value":"Has 2 BMC domains which can evolve independently of each other"}],"commentType":"DOMAIN"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"PROSITE-ProRule","id":"PRU01279"}],"value":"Belongs to the EutL/PduB family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"9278503"}],"value":"In strain MG1655 the eut operon is interrupted by the CPZ-55 prophage, encoding 9 genes situated between eutA and eutB, which are translated in the other direction. CPZ-55 may prevent expression of the eut operon in strain MG1655. Strain W3110 does not have this prophage element and should be able to express the operon"}],"commentType":"CAUTION"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":219,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Bacterial microcompartment shell protein EutL","featureId":"PRO_0000201516"},{"type":"Domain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":113,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"BMC circularly permuted 1","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"PROSITE-ProRule","id":"PRU01279"}]},{"type":"Domain","location":{"start":{"value":114,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":215,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"BMC circularly permuted 2","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"PROSITE-ProRule","id":"PRU01279"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":45,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":46,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Part of the acidic patch lining the small pore","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19451619"}]},{"type":"Binding site","location":{"start":{"value":45,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":45,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","featureCrossReferences":[{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:57603"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source